{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1418 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "801930b5941dc0e9940e89c1966838fd148612e4c193d41eacc7f6461f921fe3" :edb/db-before-t 1417 :edb/db-after-t 1418 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534730 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:18:14.120Z" 13194139534730 true] [17592186051847 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306085 13194139534730 true] [17592186051847 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306086 13194139534730 true] [17592186051847 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306086 13194139534730 true] [17592186051856 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306103 13194139534730 true] [17592186051856 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306103 13194139534730 true] [17592186051865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306128 13194139534730 true] [17592186051865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306129 13194139534730 true] [17592186051865 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306129 13194139534730 true] [17592186054346 1100 "If $A = \\{1, 1, 2,1, 3,3\\}$, then what is $\\left|A\\right|$?" 13194139534730 false] [17592186054346 1100 "If $A = \\{1, 1, 2,1, 3,3\\}$, then what is $\\left|A\\right|?$" 13194139534730 true] [17592186054346 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A$.\n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n1,\\qquad 2, \\qquad 3\n$$\n\nNote that only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\nSince $A$ has $3$ elements, its cardinality is\n\n$$\n\\mid A \\mid = 3\n$$" 13194139534730 false] [17592186054346 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n1,\\qquad 2, \\qquad 3\n$$\n\nNote that only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\nSince $A$ has $3$ elements, its cardinality is $|A| = 3.$" 13194139534730 true] [17592186054347 1100 "What is the cardinal number of $X = \\{ a, \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, b \\}?$" 13194139534730 true] [17592186054347 1100 "What is the cardinal number of $X = \\{a, \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, b \\}$?" 13194139534730 false] [17592186054347 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set $X$ is the number of elements in $X$.\n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $X{:}$\n$a, \\qquad \\emptyset, \\qquad \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\qquad \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, \\qquad b$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n- $\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}$ and $\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}$ each count as single, distinct elements of $X$.\n\nSince $X$ has $5$ elements, the cardinality is\n\n$$\n\\mid X \\mid = 5\n$$" 13194139534730 false] [17592186054347 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set $X$ is the number of elements in $X.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $X{:}$\n\n$$\na, \\qquad \\emptyset, \\qquad \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\qquad \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, \\qquad b\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\n- $\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}$ and $\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}$ each count as single, distinct elements of $X.$\n\nSince $X$ has $5$ elements, the cardinality is $|X| = 5.$" 13194139534730 true] [17592186054352 1100 "If $A = \\{10, 19, 28,\\ldots,865\\}$, then what is $\\mid A \\mid$?" 13194139534730 false] [17592186054352 1100 "If $A = \\{10, 19, 28,\\ldots,865\\},$ then what is $|A|?$" 13194139534730 true] [17592186054352 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A$.\n\nNotice that the elements of our set\n$10, \\quad 19, \\quad 28,\\quad \\ldots$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= 9 \\\\\n&= 19 - 10 \\\\\n&= 28 - 19 \\\\\n&= ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 10 + (n - 1) \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 10 + 9n - 9 \\\\\n&= 9n + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= {9n + 1: n ∈ N, 1 \\le n \\le N}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N$, we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9N + 1 &= 865 \\\\\n9N &= 864 \\\\\nN &= \\frac{864}{9} \\\\\nN &= 96\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{9n+1: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 96 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $96$ elements, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mid A \\mid = 96\n$$" 13194139534730 false] [17592186054352 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n10, \\quad 19, \\quad 28,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =9 \\\\  & =19-10 \\\\  & =28-19 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =10+(n-1)\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =10+9n-9 \\\\  & =9n+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={9n+1:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9N+1 & =865 \\\\ 9N & =864 \\\\ N & =\\frac{864}{9} \\\\ N & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{9n+1: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 96  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $96$ elements, we must have $|A| = 96.$" 13194139534730 true] [17592186054353 1100 "If $A = \\{3, 21, 147,1\\,029,\\ldots,5\\,931\\,980\\,229\\}$, then what is $\\mid A \\mid$?" 13194139534730 false] [17592186054353 1100 "If $A = \\{3, 21, 147,1\\,029,\\ldots,5\\,931\\,980\\,229\\},$ then what is $|A|?$" 13194139534730 true] [17592186054353 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A$.\n\nNotice that the elements of our set\n$3, \\quad 21, \\quad 147, \\quad 1\\,029,\\quad \\ldots$\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=7{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= 7 \\\\\n&= \\frac{21}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{147}{21} \\\\\n&= ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n$, is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 r^{n-1} = 3(7^{n-1}) = \\dfrac37(7)^n\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq N \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N$, we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{7}(7)^{N} &= 5931980229 \\\\\n7^{N} &= 13841287201 \\\\\nN &= \\log_{7} (13841287201) \\\\\nN &= (\\log_{10} (13841287201))/(\\log_{10} (7)) \\\\\nN &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 12\\right\\}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12$ elements, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mid A \\mid = 12\n$$" 13194139534730 false] [17592186054353 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3, \\quad 21, \\quad 147, \\quad 1\\,029,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=7{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =7 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{147}{21} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 r^{n-1} = 3(7^{n-1}) = \\dfrac37(7)^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq N \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}(7)^{N} & =5\\,931\\,980\\,229 \\\\ {7}^{N} & =13\\,841\\,287\\,201 \\\\ N & ={log}_{7}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201)}{{log}_{10}⁡(7)} \\\\ N & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 12\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12$ elements, we must have $|A| = 12.$" 13194139534730 true] [17592186054737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306078 13194139534730 true] [17592186054737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306087 13194139534730 true] [17592186054737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306090 13194139534730 true] [17592186054737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306130 13194139534730 true] [17592186054737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306137 13194139534730 true] [17592186054738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306093 13194139534730 true] [17592186054738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306144 13194139534730 true] [17592186054738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306151 13194139534730 true] [17592186054739 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306096 13194139534730 true] [17592186054739 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306100 13194139534730 true] [17592186054739 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306104 13194139534730 true] [17592186054739 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306108 13194139534730 true] [17592186054740 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306111 13194139534730 true] [17592186054740 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306115 13194139534730 true] [17592186054740 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306118 13194139534730 true] [17592186054740 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306122 13194139534730 true] [17592186054740 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306125 13194139534730 true] [17592186054989 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534730 true] [17592186054989 1100 "If $S = {1, {2, 3}, 4, {5, 6}}$, then $\\mid S|=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139534730 false] [17592186054989 1100 "If $S={1,{2,3},4,{5,6}}$, then $|S|=${{field-1}}." 13194139534730 true] [17592186054989 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set $S$ is the number of elements in $S.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $S:$\n\n$$\n1,\\,{2,3},\\,4,\\,{5,6}\n$$\n\nNote that ${2,3}$ and ${5,6}$ each count as single elements of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ has $4$ elements, the cardinality is $|S|=4.$" 13194139534730 true] [17592186054993 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534730 true] [17592186054993 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nIf $A = {-200,-193,-186, …, 997}$, then $\\mid A|=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"sets-defined-using-geometric-sequences\"></a>" 13194139534730 false] [17592186054993 1100 "If $A={-200,-193,-186,…,997},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." 13194139534730 true] [17592186054993 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-200,\\,-193,\\,-186,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =7 \\\\  & =-193-(-200) \\\\  & =-186-(-193) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-200+(n-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =-200+7n-7 \\\\  & =7n-207.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={7n-207:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7N-207 & =997 \\\\ 7N & =1\\,204 \\\\ N & =\\frac{1\\,204}{7} \\\\ N & =172\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={7n-207:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 172}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $172$ elements, we must have $|A|=172.$" 13194139534730 true] [17592186054997 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534730 true] [17592186054997 1100 "If $T = {{}, x, y, {v, x, {w}}}$, then $\\mid T|=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"sets-defined-using-arithmetic-sequences\"></a>" 13194139534730 false] [17592186054997 1100 "If $T={{},x,y,{v,x,{w}}},$ then $|T|=${{field-1}}." 13194139534730 true] [17592186054997 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set $T$ is the number of elements in $T.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $T: